---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';

// This page is deliberately sober. The brand carries the concept on the index;
// here the technical register carries the proof, and the two do not mix inside
// a paragraph.

const matrix = [
  {
    asset: 'Secrets',
    cells: [
      { head: 'Access', body: 'Structural — the value never enters the box.', tone: 'strong' },
      { head: 'Use', body: 'Broker and grant gate each use by host, scope and expiry.', tone: 'strong' },
      { head: 'Exfiltration', body: 'Nothing to exfiltrate.', tone: 'strong' },
    ],
  },
  {
    asset: 'Data',
    cells: [
      { head: 'Access', body: 'Bounded by the credential the broker will use. Response-side filtering is designed, not built.', tone: 'partial' },
      { head: 'Use', body: 'Unobserved inside the box — and inert until it leaves or causes an effect.', tone: 'weak' },
      { head: 'Exfiltration', body: 'One mediated egress, destination policy, per-box attribution, full audit.', tone: 'strong' },
    ],
  },
  {
    asset: 'Actions',
    cells: [
      { head: 'Access', body: 'The broker holds the capability; the agent holds a sentinel.', tone: 'strong' },
      { head: 'Use', body: 'Granted per host and purpose by a human. Intent verification is a guardrail today.', tone: 'partial' },
      { head: 'Exfiltration', body: '—', tone: 'none' },
    ],
  },
  {
    asset: 'Compute',
    cells: [
      { head: 'Access', body: 'Full, by design. Root, package installs, nested Docker.', tone: 'none' },
      { head: 'Use', body: 'Bounded by the pool resource envelope.', tone: 'strong' },
      { head: 'Exfiltration', body: 'Egress passes the proxy like everything else.', tone: 'strong' },
    ],
  },
];

const roles = [
  {
    role: 'Compiler',
    when: 'Authoring time',
    body: 'Turns a natural-language grant into policy. Runs when a human approves something, never on a request.',
  },
  {
    role: 'Sensor',
    when: 'Request time, when needed',
    body: 'Turns observed traffic into structured attributes — what this payload is, not whether to allow it.',
  },
  {
    role: 'Decider',
    when: 'Always',
    body: 'Evaluates attributes against policy. Deterministic, auditable, and never a model.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Security model — Discobox"
  description="How Discobox contains an autonomous agent: one mediated network boundary, credentials that never enter the sandbox, and an honest account of what is enforced today and what is not."
>
  <SiteHeader />

  <main class="page doc">
    <header class="doc__head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Security model</p>
      <h1 class="doc__title">What is enforced, and what is not.</h1>
      <p class="lede doc__lede">
        Discobox is in development. This page describes the architecture, states
        which parts of it run today, and names the gap between the two. Read it
        before you point a discobox at anything you care about.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="doc__section">
      <h2>Two classes of guarantee</h2>
      <p>
        Discobox makes two different kinds of promise, and conflating them would be
        dishonest.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Secrets are protected structurally.</strong> The credential is not in
        the sandbox. That holds regardless of what the agent does, what it installs,
        or whether it has been fully compromised — there is nothing there to take.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Data is protected by mediated flow.</strong> Data has to enter the box
        to be useful, so no absence claim is available. Instead every path out is
        narrow, identified, policy-checked and logged. That is a real guarantee, but
        it is a different one, and it degrades differently under attack.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc__section">
      <h2>Three doors</h2>
      <p>
        The security surface reduces to what enters, what leaves, and what changes in
        the world. What the agent does inside the box is unobserved and unconstrained,
        because in-box activity is inert: it becomes risk only by crossing one of
        those three boundaries, and each of them is a chokepoint at the network layer.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is what makes the model tractable. Policing capability is an infinite
        enumeration problem. Policing egress is a finite one.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc__section">
      <h2>The matrix</h2>
      <p>Where each asset class stands, including the weak cells.</p>

      <div class="matrix">
        {
          matrix.map((row) => (
            <div class="matrix__row">
              <h3 class="matrix__asset">{row.asset}</h3>
              <div class="matrix__cells">
                {row.cells.map((cell) => (
                  <div class:list={['matrix__cell', `matrix__cell--${cell.tone}`]}>
                    <p class="matrix__head">{cell.head}</p>
                    <p class="matrix__body">{cell.body}</p>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="doc__section" id="limitations">
      <h2>Known limitation: unattested use declaration</h2>

      <p>The credential flow works like this:</p>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>The agent asks for a credential, naming the host and describing what it needs it for.</li>
        <li>You approve, creating a grant scoped to a host, a set of uses, and an expiry.</li>
        <li>When the agent wants to use it, it declares the command it is about to run. A model checks that the command matches the approved use and returns a sentinel.</li>
        <li>The sentinel becomes a real credential only at the proxy, on an outbound request to the granted domain.</li>
      </ol>

      <div class="callout">
        <p class="callout__head">The gap</p>
        <p>
          Step 3 runs inside the sandbox, on a description the agent supplies. An agent
          that misrepresents what it is about to run can obtain a sentinel for a use you
          did not approve.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p>
        <strong>What still holds</strong>, because it is enforced outside the box: the
        credential itself never enters the sandbox, the sentinel only resolves against
        the domain in the grant, a human approved that grant, and every injection is
        audited. The gap narrows the guarantee from <em>“this specific use was
        approved”</em> to <em>“this credential was approved for this domain”</em>. It
        does not expose the credential.
      </p>
      <p>
        So this layer is currently a <strong>guardrail, not a security boundary</strong>:
        it defends against an agent that errs, not an agent that deceives.
      </p>
      <p>
        Note what that implies about the architecture. The weakest layer failing does
        not collapse the model, because the structural controls above it hold against
        both adversaries. That ordering is deliberate.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="https://github.com/discobox-ai/discobox/blob/main/docs/adr/0031-agent-credentials-are-a-portable-protocol-with-ephemeral-sentinels.md">
          ADR 0031
        </a>
        specifies the fix: move verification to the trusted side and enforce against
        observed outbound traffic rather than a declared command, making the
        declaration audit context rather than a trust boundary. That is designed and
        not yet implemented.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc__section">
      <h2>Why policy is natural language</h2>
      <p>
        Two separate arguments, and they do different jobs.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Grants generalize.</strong> One sentence covers a combinatorial space of
        concrete requests, so approval does not have to happen per call. This is what
        makes the system usable.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Judgments require comprehension.</strong> Whether a given flow violates a
        policy is semantic. “Do not send customer data to third parties” does not
        compile into a domain check or a regular expression — it depends on what is in
        the payload. This is not a convenience claim: there is no programmatic policy
        language that expresses it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Because the proxy terminates the connection, evaluation happens against the
        actual bytes on the wire rather than against the agent's account of what it is
        doing. A compromised agent can narrate anything; it cannot change what the
        proxy observed.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc__section">
      <h2>Three roles, and the model is never the decider</h2>
      <p>
        A model that renders authorization verdicts is an attack surface and an
        auditing problem. The design keeps it out of that position.
      </p>

      <div class="roles">
        {
          roles.map((role) => (
            <div class="card role">
              <h3 class="role__name">{role.role}</h3>
              <p class="role__when">{role.when}</p>
              <p class="role__body">{role.body}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <p class="roles__note">
        Natural language is the source of truth for policy; the deterministic artifact
        compiled from it is what runs. Compiled policy is generated and never
        hand-edited, the same discipline this repository already applies to its
        generated API code. <span class="tag tag--designed">Designed</span>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc__section">
      <h2>What Discobox does not defend against</h2>
      <ul class="limits">
        <li>
          <strong>Covert channels.</strong> Data smuggled through an allowed destination —
          a commit message, an issue body — in a channel you legitimately use. Bounded,
          attributable and logged, not prevented.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Payloads the proxy cannot read.</strong> An agent that encrypts before
          sending defeats content inspection. Treating unintelligibility as a policy
          input is designed; it is not built.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A determined human attacker with sandbox access.</strong> The isolation
          is VM-backed, but the threat model this is built for is an autonomous agent
          that errs or is hijacked, not a targeted adversary attacking the hypervisor.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Anything downstream of an approved action.</strong> If you grant a
          credential that can delete production, the grant is the control. Scope
          accordingly.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <p class="doc__foot">
      Found something wrong here?
      <a href="https://github.com/discobox-ai/discobox/issues">Open an issue</a>.
    </p>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .doc {
    padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4rem) clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5rem);
  }

  .doc__head {
    max-width: 62ch;
  }

  .doc__title {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem);
  }

  .doc__lede {
    margin-top: 1.2rem;
    max-width: 62ch;
  }

  .doc__section {
    margin-top: clamp(2.75rem, 6vw, 4rem);
    max-width: 68ch;
  }

  .doc__section h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.35rem, 2.6vw, 1.7rem);
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .doc__section p {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .doc__section strong {
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: 650;
  }

  .doc__section em {
    color: var(--text);
    font-style: normal;
  }

  /* Matrix ---------------------------------------------------------------- */

  .matrix {
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.25rem;
  }

  .matrix__asset {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--magenta);
  }

  .matrix__cells {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    margin-top: 0.7rem;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
  }

  @media (min-width: 46rem) {
    .matrix__cells {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }

  .matrix__cell {
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 0.85rem 0.9rem;
    border-left-width: 3px;
  }

  .matrix__cell--strong {
    border-left-color: var(--green);
  }

  .matrix__cell--partial {
    border-left-color: var(--gold);
  }

  .matrix__cell--weak {
    border-left-color: var(--salmon);
  }

  .matrix__head {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-faint);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
  }

  .matrix__body {
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  /* Steps, callout, roles, limits ----------------------------------------- */

  .steps {
    margin: 1.2rem 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  .callout {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--salmon);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--salmon) 7%, var(--ink-sunken));
    padding: 1.1rem 1.2rem;
  }

  .callout__head {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--salmon);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .callout p + p {
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
  }

  .roles {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
  }

  @media (min-width: 46rem) {
    .roles {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }

  .role__name {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }

  .role__when {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--magenta);
    margin-top: 0.2rem;
  }

  .role__body {
    margin-top: 0.7rem;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .roles__note {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .limits {
    margin: 1.2rem 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.85rem;
  }

  .doc__foot {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    color: var(--text-faint);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }
</style>
